\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431e3f50-9697-534f-b81d-9a9818b4b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D + \\frac{x^{6}}{15} + \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496447} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7877ce-f2f4-5a6c-bc6c-70bfe99570ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496452} {:answer/id #uuid "84ba00bd-ae03-5a27-bcc9-49ecdcd0d954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496462} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2b08ce-e008-5a63-b350-3f4ff774087b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496469} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b497df-f5b4-5a27-8162-1ef52bf3b6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f7877ce-f2f4-5a6c-bc6c-70bfe99570ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496452} :answer-field/id #uuid "553b156e-b41f-5b74-a709-a7550e26968b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9b05e0-1d53-595d-87f6-fcb0ea344f67" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 6 x^{4} - 24 x^{3} - 6 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-8)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)) + (\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c97bc6-f60d-54c4-b7a0-0109b8b665a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496442} {:answer/id #uuid "52effbc3-75dd-51c0-8b3c-224c3cd54fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496453} {:answer/id #uuid "706eaced-8e8c-52e2-bfea-e59cc1590246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496458} {:answer/id #uuid "96fe4e2b-4cff-525b-8ad1-4de8638750aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496466} {:answer/id #uuid "d82f188a-2305-57a9-8ca5-cb043c85d10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "706eaced-8e8c-52e2-bfea-e59cc1590246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496458} :answer-field/id #uuid "e387b32c-b692-5d4f-ab95-49c805f1cfd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "adfc6c39-9c84-5054-86dc-08ce214860eb" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=24 x^{3} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((6)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((6)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-12)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((-12)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + ((4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481097fd-f9ae-55d3-8f5c-722e350c0b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496450} {:answer/id #uuid "49e876e9-eec7-5526-ad6f-32142b42b519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496451} {:answer/id #uuid "55ca0676-4986-53de-8bfe-c98830d32367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496455} {:answer/id #uuid "8922d260-2923-5acd-b341-65d16531156a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D + \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496463} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2e2fff-1806-5d13-b4de-becd24629d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ca0676-4986-53de-8bfe-c98830d32367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C x + D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496455} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad2a396-3caa-5aaa-b023-a60543b5a4e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f11b903b-958d-5c91-a4bb-2c7b220c4cab" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 16 x^{4} - 12 x^{3} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((2)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((1)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)) + ((1)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32326d-f8f7-50a3-985e-b1e1d37cdc2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{2}}{3} + 4 e^{4 x} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}}{18}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496492} {:answer/id #uuid "1126b502-7a36-55b9-9de4-2391d8cb1c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{2 x}{3} + e^{4 x} + \\frac{2}{x - 2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496493} {:answer/id #uuid "38d904cc-8188-5c23-8d1a-2ce92740dcf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{x^{2}}{3} - \\frac{e^{4 x}}{4} - 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496504} {:answer/id #uuid "478bff4b-1f38-511e-8a4f-a99b90f1c64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{e^{4 x}}{4} + 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496508} {:answer/id #uuid "ccda3c2e-69cf-54f3-a968-ffd12e0b8c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{e^{4 x}}{4} + 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "478bff4b-1f38-511e-8a4f-a99b90f1c64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{e^{4 x}}{4} + 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496508} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31a20d1-c2da-5d37-9f86-6795a15d4026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b7e5e08-6941-58d2-a73a-e51fbed395c6" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $-3f'(x)=- \\frac{6}{x - 2}- 3 e^{4 x}+2 x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int (\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int \\left(- \\frac{6}{x - 2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (- 3 e^{4 x})\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (2 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 6 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{3 e^{4 x}}{4}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+\\frac{e^{4 x}}{4}- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ef27c0-c2ac-5049-b6d0-b6c6c30dbda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x^{2} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ef27c0-c2ac-5049-b6d0-b6c6c30dbda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x^{2} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496584} :answer-field/id #uuid "7761440a-3e1f-5b63-807b-471d79bc3612" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "055460a6-0f9b-526b-8dc4-1d9477fd930a" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y'=- 6 x- 2 e^{- 2 x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-4.$\n\n$y=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ny'=- 6 x- 2 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y'\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 6 x- 2 e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (- 6 x)\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 2 e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-6)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right) + (-2)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ & =- 3 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=- 3 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=-4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =(- 3 (0)^{2}) + (e^{- 2 (0)})+C \\\\ -4 & =(0) + (1)+C \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=- 3 x^{2} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44d307e3-3971-5b44-8870-ec07964729c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496560} {:answer/id #uuid "6309219f-5921-50af-8d03-ceca3f9cd7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=4 x^{2} - e^{2 x} + 2 \\cos{(x )} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496565} {:answer/id #uuid "6d39cea8-f729-59fd-932b-b421f7c81e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} + 2 \\sin{(x )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496566} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf52bf4-503f-518b-8224-990dfc17f0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} + 2 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496578} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a8c8a0-b258-57db-8417-7f4ed7dd5e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{4} + \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bf52bf4-503f-518b-8224-990dfc17f0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} + 2 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496578} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e4d76e-d5c8-50ce-b204-2238bbc1ebf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28dcb28d-1db1-599d-9052-a43879b7d29d" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=4 x^{2}- e^{2 x}+2 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=4 x^{2}- e^{2 x}+2 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4 x^{2}- e^{2 x}+2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (4 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(4)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) + (-1)\\left(\\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}\\right) + (2)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+2 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+2 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\left(\\frac{4 (0)^{3}}{3}\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{e^{2 (0)}}{2}\\right) + (2 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -2 & =(0) + \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =- \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} + 2 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7a616ea-75de-59d6-9be8-1efebdcb8ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} + e^{x} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7a616ea-75de-59d6-9be8-1efebdcb8ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} + e^{x} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496587} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb17feec-f119-512b-bbc3-1c49567bcc7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "434627b4-46f1-5eea-a26e-ab4e978bb4fe" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $f'(x)=- 2 x^{2}+e^{x}+3 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $f(0)=-1.$\n\n$f(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 2 x^{2}+e^{x}+3 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 2 x^{2}+e^{x}+3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\int (- 2 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (e^{x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) + (1)(e^{x}) + (3)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3}+e^{x}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3}+e^{x}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0)=-1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =\\left(- \\frac{2 (0)^{3}}{3}\\right) + (e^{(0)}) + (3 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -1 & =(0) + (1) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} + e^{x} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bbbb9c-5bf7-5e11-a192-421ca39a2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496575} {:answer/id #uuid "32a5418a-1e9b-4a09-b939-e21f730687f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^2+e^{-x}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89bbbb9c-5bf7-5e11-a192-421ca39a2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496575} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a9991b-4a5a-59bb-8999-d08fed4a1afe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "540280c5-2fb8-54b1-88f4-aa5261ba0b12" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=- 4 x- e^{- x}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-5.$\n\n$g(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=- 4 x- e^{- x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (- 4 x)\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right) + (-1)(- e^{- x})+C \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2}+e^{- x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=- 2 x^{2}+e^{- x}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-5$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =(- 2 (0)^{2}) + (e^{- (0)})+C \\\\ -5 & =(0) + (1)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 6 + e^{- x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd041ec-76f5-576c-b5f5-4ac1e33a875d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496567} {:answer/id #uuid "71be9bd2-467c-544f-a86c-b94537352986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{17}{2} + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496569} {:answer/id #uuid "7a56cab2-0b3b-50dc-8fac-bc8339e5496a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496572} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c9a11e-69a9-5c57-a016-135f4ee342f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- 4 x^{2} + 3 \\cos{(x )} - 12 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496581} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e5a3f6-fd19-5031-bd08-3342f142ba83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{2 x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\sin{(x )}}{2} - \\frac{31}{4} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dd041ec-76f5-576c-b5f5-4ac1e33a875d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496567} :answer-field/id #uuid "94031bbf-31fa-5e35-83b6-b4b9cede32c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7efbb8af-b76c-5365-a359-23b71e692cfb" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $f'(x)=- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $f(0)=-8.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x^{2}+e^{- 2 x}+3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\int (- 4 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) + (1)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right) + (3)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}+3 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0)=-8$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =\\left(- \\frac{4 (0)^{3}}{3}\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 (0)}}{2}\\right) + (3 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -8 & =(0) + \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =- \\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} + 3 \\sin{(x )} - \\frac{15}{2} - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544429ba-196d-56ee-8bbe-1db7b23b9340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "544429ba-196d-56ee-8bbe-1db7b23b9340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496563} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b77f0f8-1f53-5123-abd8-4fb977688cb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b0a6a93-71df-5af7-be4e-1bb4244efcb6" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-5.$\n\n$g(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 4 x^{2}- e^{- x}- 3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (- 4 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{- x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 3 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-4)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) + (-1)(- e^{- x}) + (-3)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}+e^{- x}- 3 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3}+e^{- x}- 3 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-5$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =\\left(- \\frac{4 (0)^{3}}{3}\\right) + (e^{- (0)}) + (- 3 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -5 & =(0) + (1) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=- \\frac{4 x^{3}}{3} - 3 \\sin{(x )} - 6 + e^{- x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8f3b58-8858-5461-8c2f-f3fd0569f838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=- 2 x^{2} + \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496556} {:answer/id #uuid "57d4f7d9-ed6c-5a97-87e2-d6c53f06b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496564} {:answer/id #uuid "7694c46f-e60d-5d4f-a7ce-be776be3d5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496570} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cac29f-14b5-5870-adf5-b3a40432c52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=4 x - e^{2 x} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496588} {:answer/id #uuid "f021c66e-4c17-5d8b-b6b0-3bdb846a1345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{4} - \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d4f7d9-ed6c-5a97-87e2-d6c53f06b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496564} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9b219b-9380-5027-899f-fba5ea430d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afdd11d0-4e3c-5120-9f08-464acb94efd0" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $g'(x)=4 x- e^{2 x}$ that satisfies the condition $g(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ng'(x)=4 x- e^{2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int g'(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4 x- e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ g(x) & =\\int (4 x)\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(4)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right) + (-1)\\left(\\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ & =2 x^{2}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $g(x)=2 x^{2}- \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $g(0)=-2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(2 (0)^{2}) + \\left(- \\frac{e^{2 (0)}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ -2 & =(0) + \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ C & =- \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ng(x)=2 x^{2} - \\frac{e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46a51229-760d-59b7-9713-62d0167e9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a51229-760d-59b7-9713-62d0167e9710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496561} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54af8b6-64e9-50e8-a679-eed9f7d39cf4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff8b0291-a711-56fa-8ba2-9a74a6a7ab1c" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y'=- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-4.$\n\n$y=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative is already isolated, so we have\n\n$$\ny'=- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y'\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (- 6 x^{2}- 2 e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (- 6 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 2 e^{- 2 x})\\,\\text{d}x + \\int (- 2 \\cos{(x )})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =(-6)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) + (-2)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right) + (-2)(\\sin{(x )})+C \\\\ & =- 2 x^{3}+e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\sin{(x )}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=- 2 x^{3}+e^{- 2 x}- 2 \\sin{(x )}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=-4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =(- 2 (0)^{3}) + (e^{- 2 (0)}) + (- 2 \\sin{((0) )})+C \\\\ -4 & =(0) + (1) + (0)+C \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=- 2 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{(x )} - 5 + e^{- 2 x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496747} {:answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496784} {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496976} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcde23-8fb4-54be-b4f9-12b3458b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8a9980-7371-5df1-8410-26fa8e6ff9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496683} {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2fd99-6f60-54d4-ba6c-934c44c7e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d46346-4c74-57e4-aaa3-613d6a4a40ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f217825-c491-59e7-9325-ea7e5e5d43ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344285" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496755} :answer-field/id #uuid "2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166840" :question/problem "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\nThe pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $\\left(\\frac{5x+3}{2}\\right)$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496666} {:answer/id #uuid "44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496806} {:answer/id #uuid "68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496883} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497061} {:answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497094} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95012" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{6}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -13 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{13}{6} & \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{2}{x}_{2} &=-13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}{x}_{2} &=\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second, third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462d45d4-2587-5767-bbda-82dc90eb9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496809} {:answer/id #uuid "480be999-cd10-5a4f-83f5-02eb7f7b0275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496819} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0f4e40-8515-583c-aa83-8de5f9788688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a94d15-fe13-5d85-9f7d-69d7a8a6ed69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed7b5966-6097-5c94-afc7-cde3e72271b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344323" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5063f8a0-899b-5e4f-b70e-5fbeab173e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496834} {:answer/id #uuid "cf933d00-1cd0-58a3-b054-9a9b5f189803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497086} {:answer/id #uuid "df8562cf-2270-52ce-9299-2a3db48074d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497137} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e14dc5-18ed-5023-8fe2-0751bffc21d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497158} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3568be-b178-5f3b-b7ec-36b1e63a80e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8562cf-2270-52ce-9299-2a3db48074d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497137} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6ba6fef-e5a6-577a-86ab-a507607e574c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8afba66-d1aa-558e-99f9-1c7592f06bb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131232" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2\\sqrt{2}t,\\,4⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}),\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(4t)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}(4-{t}^{2}))}^{2}+(-4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+2{(4-{t}^{2})}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+(32-16{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4})+16{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{4}+16{t}^{2}+32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+4}\\right)}{{t}^{2}+4}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e6265d-627c-5bfe-b4a4-c9064cb498a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497959} {:answer/id #uuid "460eaa64-2244-54be-b725-8a2f2bf21ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497960} {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd59b4d-9677-5eda-a75f-2e81639dd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498097} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf3078c-b7b8-5e5f-a2a5-c5b18a6edb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} :answer-field/id #uuid "6accb65d-487d-5e4c-9f58-e9f7512e092a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157c2ce3-5327-5267-a129-7f9aebf9b49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115560" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{k}{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} & =k \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=2$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e41d7eb-d6b5-5b2c-946b-12009ab1f71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498095} {:answer/id #uuid "9784723c-c70e-51dd-9e53-5d403867e4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498143} {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} {:answer/id #uuid "b803ed2a-92df-50aa-86b4-a74a3d347a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e978c6-725e-52b7-a7d5-e19dd3beea3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} :answer-field/id #uuid "7738aed2-6969-583c-b1d2-159e3752029b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19618a93-f5c7-57b8-b5e4-4ba96b1ca9c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51816" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =k \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{15}{3}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e78bfe-9b51-5648-bc1f-b326bed0ae1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498005} {:answer/id #uuid "978e1395-42f6-59da-be0b-d636897c86b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4844" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498145} {:answer/id #uuid "b232cea5-5b98-5d1e-aef9-7f111da7c9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498197} {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6d116c-6828-5758-80e7-52fc119e9bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac1216a-a2d9-5440-8a5e-99b9a41b6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498286} :answer-field/id #uuid "effd6099-f4df-5d9b-a019-343ae34a858c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bd1cff1-6c69-5338-8c54-dfce19778dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-152011" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0651e04-cc61-5bcf-af3b-f91d6f9a0b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498158} {:answer/id #uuid "ac362cb6-492d-5473-b6dc-d32427af7ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498179} {:answer/id #uuid "db06cb6f-0562-5273-a873-d0e8e28fdf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498288} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb9f323-6fcc-523f-9e46-a8019383f7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{4},-1)$, radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498294} {:answer/id #uuid "f048c8b8-0deb-58cc-8917-7eb188230fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac362cb6-492d-5473-b6dc-d32427af7ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498179} :answer-field/id #uuid "d763b4a3-5d4a-5059-b073-6cac4252989e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d79c8724-85a2-50fb-b60d-a80008511394" :question/math-academy-id "q-103893" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y=\\frac{11}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and its radius is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "617286e8-47ce-51b6-b506-3b48cff8cb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498029} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd99149-aedb-5119-a535-5559ad7a90f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498093} {:answer/id #uuid "884b8e65-9503-5538-83b3-f52c4b72fe28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+2\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498114} {:answer/id #uuid "89575744-0951-5e71-9a75-60b9e81bb12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+4\\,\\text{d}b+\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498116} {:answer/id #uuid "cab3f89e-5562-5702-943e-6433f814978e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab3f89e-5562-5702-943e-6433f814978e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498250} :answer-field/id #uuid "361b4f31-81d9-5f43-b63b-1aa713ee6c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1b5df81-7853-5e90-b2a0-96b74729c9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79749" :question/problem "Given a right triangular prism, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ if the height is $h=1$ and the base is a right triangle with legs $a=2$ and $b=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a given right triangular prism is $V=\\frac{abh}{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂a}\\right)\\,\\text{d}a+\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂b}\\right)\\,\\text{d}b+\\left(\\frac{∂V}{∂h}\\right)\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{bh}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{ah}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{ab}{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $h=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,4,1) & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{2\\cdot 4}{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{d}a+\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c97c988-3336-5cff-9def-ec7413102202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(u+w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} {:answer/id #uuid "1c002a85-7881-5d55-b3a3-c0858f2a52d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(u+v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498789} {:answer/id #uuid "449afe6a-6893-523f-8969-4d9facf23e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498886} {:answer/id #uuid "5760564c-6d75-5aa4-a9ab-7873348de3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u-w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498930} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc9fd42-c83a-5ec5-bb24-9d73b0ba363e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c97c988-3336-5cff-9def-ec7413102202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(u+w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} :answer-field/id #uuid "219a7147-3bde-5244-974f-5c2639b194f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21557ab9-79b3-55a0-a104-51c3d273599e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161868" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23c71b1-938d-5c3c-896d-64a1b9e36fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499197} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ead61a9-5da2-511e-a657-d00ea95b48b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d1e7c48-2675-551e-94d8-a9622a621313" :question/math-academy-id "q-345097" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{42}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{144}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34df0886-f589-5a77-91fd-b574dff15d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498850} {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} {:answer/id #uuid "669efc65-a593-55a2-9cb2-e9832fd6adb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498959} {:answer/id #uuid "9d702685-480a-51e1-930a-6e01b50056fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0037796-de78-55f7-b178-e17d9506d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd8c6c1-d268-5074-8c20-054b800150f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82157" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\left(-\\frac{a}{2x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{2x}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{2x}\\right)} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{\\left(-\\frac{a}{2x}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{a}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17418eda-e303-583e-abb8-8163f34cf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa17f5a-3932-5a85-be4a-9e7f8aca50c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499103} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d5967-0e62-5e9c-80be-bb9563606efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499116} {:answer/id #uuid "e507ffc4-50dc-56ba-8db0-2ce573a2305a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} :answer-field/id #uuid "06575685-8b6e-5313-b708-d919a2eb705b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-82910" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bd8942-8ea6-5f03-b9c2-980c08387095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}+{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498858} {:answer/id #uuid "3f95ea7f-6aac-57fb-89fc-e3618b1effd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498876} {:answer/id #uuid "472465ce-5697-565a-b045-a23526f9815c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi /3}-{e}^{-2\\pi /3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498893} {:answer/id #uuid "a7929dc7-bc71-5a90-8bb2-c5a85e81928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} {:answer/id #uuid "e97e245e-6898-5e7c-8a99-3baaa90c0589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{3\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7929dc7-bc71-5a90-8bb2-c5a85e81928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499099} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f08aa4-9c3b-5881-9036-fcd624473552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "657b78ff-bfd8-5861-b182-c1815c474783" :question/math-academy-id "q-9534" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2{e}^{3\\theta }$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{e}^{3\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{3\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{3\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{6\\theta }+36{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{40{e}^{6\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}{e}^{3\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{3}({e}^{3\\pi /2}-{e}^{-3\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498946} {:answer/id #uuid "65e56828-40a4-5c88-88b8-dc7bcfb344e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498957} {:answer/id #uuid "74d13041-41ad-5e74-9ece-8544d7624726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498988} {:answer/id #uuid "ae716747-adb4-5e5e-89d4-b2a39710996d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499114} {:answer/id #uuid "b83a0cfa-c6af-5ba3-83d9-9e7b4d506e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e7704d1-16de-5f57-9381-77fa91a75339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "I(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" :question/math-academy-id "q-345156" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ 2 &=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b4ef9-8eb6-5c5c-b433-5ffc4c7236ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498824} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c6c00-5b20-53bc-acb7-b0b31ab2298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498947} {:answer/id #uuid "88676aa6-a08b-583a-a6f9-2618bd726361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499034} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} {:answer/id #uuid "f378af41-a686-5292-9362-123eea7e70ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecc3609-91c7-5f66-a205-ec1648ad9bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77ed6de7-60cd-57e5-95ef-8b1b61a0f9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160638" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/61/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1784c964-2bd8-551a-9e62-6cb6f2560ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498780} {:answer/id #uuid "255b1548-0862-5d3a-8e4b-d22ea5aa0f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498812} {:answer/id #uuid "4f74f069-41cb-54fc-8a9a-08d22d7c1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498916} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9780c4-afc1-59e7-ac65-84b80ad23ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} {:answer/id #uuid "903bd708-b320-50fc-b991-e52faae4df28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d9780c4-afc1-59e7-ac65-84b80ad23ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} :answer-field/id #uuid "2030cf44-2880-55da-b8da-7f5dffbc7894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787730fc-5924-57cd-ac3a-f6e87e085583" :question/math-academy-id "q-161893" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n∭(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8fe484-3889-58d1-8f1f-caa851dbee11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498835} {:answer/id #uuid "40230e03-b9ac-5a57-8432-36ead7d33681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498877} {:answer/id #uuid "686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498966} {:answer/id #uuid "b10740ac-3d00-5d70-a025-0645fac307c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499123} {:answer/id #uuid "e06340a0-7e4c-5772-a063-3ea903fbcb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686a3a2f-5456-57f4-bf33-92993d1268ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498966} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b67e92f-d71e-5f0f-ba51-6ad73ad30d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fcf62b8-f859-5393-a4f9-53904a00d462" :question/math-academy-id "q-345133" :question/problem "![](images/12/12a73555c78b324406714337b999b12a2166559eb1795236854e29747e88a5b1.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (4\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-6\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{13},$ $\\beta =\\frac{2}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{3}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+2=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498842} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb97491-dba6-5589-9683-9f48dd09761c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498948} {:answer/id #uuid "a1aa3515-ef91-55c3-b692-ae8e2eb1e1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499075} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb0366a-9b57-5e7e-bef8-5e55731b9195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499178} {:answer/id #uuid "d1098f1c-859a-50c4-99fe-1eb8a2a4e374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308ed31c-23c6-5076-9326-a4c8719125ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498842} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c02197-355c-50c6-989b-28bd28b9ed42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80b3a60d-0575-5aee-af41-4b72c4744aed" :question/math-academy-id "q-345106" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58cde9ddaa098a14ebb26bb70128f018f0c5892214815cc2d3057c9881a9546f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0989efb5-1038-59ac-8680-60117382e68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498746} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce9d6eb-3f8f-56eb-a2d0-a4cfacaf7268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498792} {:answer/id #uuid "38d06719-2aef-52ec-b523-72bbe2d5ce07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498855} {:answer/id #uuid "51cc30b4-2098-5551-9641-1f9551e409a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} {:answer/id #uuid "52b44f14-c340-50a7-9cae-50d08c357aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51cc30b4-2098-5551-9641-1f9551e409a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498920} :answer-field/id #uuid "f107053f-9248-55df-a0bd-cf3499751023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d77d15-6969-5117-952d-e2b202d58514" :question/math-academy-id "q-15123" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12666955-acda-50b1-a759-415719031788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498766} {:answer/id #uuid "33260567-19af-5f87-8391-f7b7b20532c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498847} {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} {:answer/id #uuid "c17ed11d-1615-505b-9ee3-f62b086faf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499157} {:answer/id #uuid "faf10f5c-fe15-5f32-843c-38243f61e787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} :answer-field/id #uuid "772a5b09-0846-503c-9495-bc958a42c8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92990ecc-5ba0-56e3-944d-6e84f299eb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-162210" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-{e}^{x},\\,v=y,\\,w=z{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & 0 & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4a70e6b-af1c-5577-8af3-126ce8b4aabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499246} :answer-field/id #uuid "6345212c-d6d1-5b93-bb17-d2f3f24d44d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a920ee2-be49-53c0-8384-6f4e796b9132" :question/math-academy-id "q-345126" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{16}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/23/23e8796d3977632278d4d393510a30df974634750a1c02f56fd8bacbe3b5d57a.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I=32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/16)}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=8\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+32\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+16q=12.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n8(0)+32(0)+16q=12,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n16q=12\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{4}$ coulombs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cdb946-258e-53a2-b8f8-2ab57e235521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498813} {:answer/id #uuid "5740c8e7-55d8-5593-be17-37b3da74f6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498929} {:answer/id #uuid "7432af23-f90c-521c-82d6-f4de37bca049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498985} {:answer/id #uuid "e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499242} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc04d2-ee9b-5d88-9e30-1df86999a8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+0.1q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39afebc-fd32-5996-9855-d2bef0af1a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499242} :answer-field/id #uuid "762f659e-79b7-5a6e-95e6-304faf42e2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cd80162-3ef8-51ba-9ca2-79d0022c1e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-345111" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f6c545a697bfa58794995f802950e509e3df63bd3d34db957ee3556461ec03f.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.025\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.025}=40q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+40q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040577a1-f488-586f-a244-5ac52452a051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498738} {:answer/id #uuid "134f7fbf-b645-5f5e-a680-2cf1d5fc853c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498772} {:answer/id #uuid "7649b34b-2218-533c-b3c5-146847fddc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498997} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} {:answer/id #uuid "e83877aa-f978-5e05-a20d-0069c96a2685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} :answer-field/id #uuid "612abc3f-006a-54a9-ba2c-ed70e2955dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad40770e-3352-5877-ade7-ccfd965beccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162185" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-y,\\,v=x,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f76ca2e-6813-528d-b05a-1cbae894cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498798} {:answer/id #uuid "31287e2e-19cf-5f18-8334-37c5ad1ac19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498843} {:answer/id #uuid "38d08ad7-d775-5da0-9791-be3349a2c3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498856} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498872} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cecd57-7009-5476-b23e-18da5400c0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ddc27d2-9734-50a3-b245-b012f402c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498872} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9234df-4595-59b2-aa42-20eb5e74670b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb8dd05b-77ee-5cc1-8581-cca32ddb619d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345145" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e03b65f8301526be5ff2ce5ec3c7f8b896906bfef2a6308d2125e93f0ecf631.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{36}{145}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{32}{145}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160e2a0c-f074-5874-a5f2-17cc1fc2cae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498777} {:answer/id #uuid "6d20f288-51ab-5e95-ad79-fa5df49f89a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498973} {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} {:answer/id #uuid "c87b6998-b8e9-5934-9893-be14c9c09927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499169} {:answer/id #uuid "d102d768-01d6-510f-bd39-ea2452eac6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a35283a7-4adc-5de1-826d-87a387cd4479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499081} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafcbac5-e575-5e1d-93e5-5e527facd2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d774fdc1-484c-5e5e-b140-afbc35a348d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51202" :question/problem "![](images/87/87a3563153fe0310767a79a96df0df5f6c0824027776b761df4a792b8b9b235e.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465a9a31-c9a7-5242-b06e-83d1de732242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498892} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea53a6a-43cc-5424-b0c0-f4959baa4754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{7}}{32{v}^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498913} {:answer/id #uuid "6842d70f-c788-545a-96c5-2eb791e9c865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{5}}{16{v}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498965} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cdef2e-ff7f-5848-8193-597d13abef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{6}}{8{v}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499100} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5a67c3-eb72-52e8-8d6e-906807a56f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{2{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465a9a31-c9a7-5242-b06e-83d1de732242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498892} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4acd8be-661d-5d96-83bb-5a718612e8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0110074-4c62-517f-b5cb-ff4cff4e0579" :question/math-academy-id "q-22838" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\left(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}}\\right)}^{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{-2{v}^{4}}\\right)}^{5} &= \\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule in the numerator and the power of product rule in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({u}^{2})}^{5}}{{(-2{v}^{4})}^{5}} &= \\frac{{u}^{2\\cdot 5}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {({v}^{4})}^{5}} &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-2)^{5}\\cdot {v}^{4\\cdot 5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{{u}^{10}}{(-32)\\cdot {v}^{20}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{{u}^{10}}{32{v}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdfeeca-c3d7-5d09-9a71-f864d8a8b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498800} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6b0d94-eb76-52a5-b259-da50f984cfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498943} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a46c95-6fde-5fa7-8bc5-e88f21d169ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499120} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499166} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd8c843-c34b-5a57-b6ff-ef052ba33dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff2020-1de0-5813-9759-f90d2787076d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499166} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d65df-34cc-548f-ad19-ac000012253d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2a6b6c4-abd9-5fd2-be45-a09229653766" :question/math-academy-id "q-345151" :question/problem "![](images/72/725398d9e534fbc888ef9963c9075538aa70cde9317c664b253394fb63cff052.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta &=\\frac{16}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{72}{125}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{24}{125}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498874} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a4fe9-8db6-57d0-b8ed-faa4b47a685e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499128} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b404c5-802e-5a1a-a90d-3dcb65998bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499238} {:answer/id #uuid "f97b8d8b-bc73-57de-b5fa-e8f9bbe0efe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499290} {:answer/id #uuid "fb95efed-9536-58af-96fd-52fa8b8b750c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8b8abf-8932-5377-9152-a6b4049fa212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498874} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ecb4ea2-b9c4-5db0-9f41-8f3cda9910e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f652ccf1-f487-574b-9e94-0f12f4efc494" :question/math-academy-id "q-345098" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $60\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/e1/e13f3dd8d881385444b215f62e0c43d6eb40d36e4b6af5f4792cba0502eb2ea2.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344607" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate is the diagonal of the square decreasing when the diagonal is $20\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-10.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $20\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $h=20.$ Substituting this information into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal is decreasing at $0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499814} {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499933} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500076} {:answer/id #uuid "df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" :question/math-academy-id "q-2777" :question/problem "![](images/19/199f1c7e18eb0c25bf7caafb8e2e71fa3e0165af81083ea57c1fc16a74935ab3.png)\n\nA square's diagonals decrease at the rate of  $2\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the diagonals are $3\\text{ m}$ long each?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=3.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 3=-6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} {:answer/id #uuid "a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499994} {:answer/id #uuid "c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500051} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-48375" :question/problem "![](images/97/97a7d465f8412c88367ed3cf148af3ce387441284c10e3f84888c0186fa597ce.png)\n\nPeter wants to cut a piece of square-shaped glass while maintaining its shape. The area of this glass is decreasing at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate are the diagonals of the glass decreasing if the length of these diagonals is $50\\text{ in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=50.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot (-1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1.5}{50} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the glass are decreasing at a rate of $0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f0116c-d24c-501d-a2e3-6d64ae73f071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499660} {:answer/id #uuid "4f094944-ced0-5da6-beff-36966c6a9bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499804} {:answer/id #uuid "5899b9f1-4d49-51f2-8d77-aac18ae3041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499825} {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd61b0-026b-5712-a0a0-1b3c6315b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacf0a3-cce6-5d50-befc-250fcc891555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94724c66-b6e2-52f7-8122-f7efcf9307aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111515" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${v}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 3=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052} {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520802} :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}\\left(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499777} :answer-field/id #uuid "d025d04b-35c6-550c-86ed-3b8dbfd3c675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-166811" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact area of ​​each wall is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The exact area of  ​each wall is ${\\left(\\frac{17}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${\\left(\\frac{17}{4}\\right)}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{17}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{17}{4}\\times \\frac{17}{4}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\times 17}{4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{17}{4}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{289}{16}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3811dc15-f044-5933-86e0-b81332887411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500581} {:answer/id #uuid "575b1543-5913-5111-9e9c-4cf207869497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500645} {:answer/id #uuid "5e277c6c-dcd2-5491-b1f3-c7fa6def4dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500660} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c8e6c-8875-5f05-83c5-ba640b983ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500687} {:answer/id #uuid "be36abcb-60b4-5b59-8d60-819c63d7b780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be36abcb-60b4-5b59-8d60-819c63d7b780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4374709-dd94-564d-a093-c34e0f6d470b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e54e6a-cd0f-51b4-97b3-2489c07dd2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97574" :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d7cc13-045d-5dfc-8f21-557e9035974d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61d7cc13-045d-5dfc-8f21-557e9035974d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60t^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9181ccb7-fad9-5675-90e4-730835fd6807" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e9395c-263f-51bd-b6a4-d472df228bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e9395c-263f-51bd-b6a4-d472df228bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{t^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500636} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2548aa-0d72-568a-936f-6eb2f6da4f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3300ef54-fdde-5f40-809c-0c4bab2d0166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3300ef54-fdde-5f40-809c-0c4bab2d0166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0de4306-091e-529b-ae8c-530f65ea903f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cc15b80-72fe-59a5-9238-583abab2565e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350802" :question/problem "Determine ${f}^{″}(t)$ given that $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\sqrt{t} \\\\ {e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -2{t}^{6}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{″}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{1-2t})\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{6})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ -2{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -12{t}^{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{1-2t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12{t}^{5})\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3/2}} \\\\ 4{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -60{t}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7b9ffa-61e8-5834-acc7-13454c3bc5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c7b9ffa-61e8-5834-acc7-13454c3bc5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500619} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc60e61-7c99-51fb-8ae6-518b9c8d435c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18e9c87c-d5d7-52d0-922d-e65af5fe041b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171258" :question/problem "Express ${\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{-1}$ as an improper fraction in its simplest form.\n\n${\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression ${\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{9}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20969546-8cf5-58bf-9f69-aa85c2b994ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500533} {:answer/id #uuid "4346ba55-161e-55f0-af3a-5ede323b90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{83}{4}+{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500596} {:answer/id #uuid "a7969ead-7a86-53c0-8879-105a6fba4435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500813} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca5c4a-5384-5160-a988-e4dc1ad1f8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500944} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a58e43-e215-570e-85ca-38eb345f6b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "464780e0-2b69-5233-beb8-cdc5f928ae62" :question/math-academy-id "q-142072" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc5889c-f956-5805-828b-a07b04225e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500725} {:answer/id #uuid "805a1efe-9356-5170-a269-8ed6c903aa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500733} {:answer/id #uuid "d93ffed7-ac55-5ecb-a0d9-3189b1b4cd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500929} {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} {:answer/id #uuid "f499ebac-94ab-572a-a984-e07f21d31ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} :answer-field/id #uuid "28822793-1aa6-5555-91d5-42b23da22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517afe53-217c-5ede-9811-b9bf164398f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140380" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05373043-ede4-5f3b-ab00-6c5eb28bd928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500476} {:answer/id #uuid "43f6eebb-f90b-5e3e-bf7f-5a7b4d0e7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500601} {:answer/id #uuid "510f8649-056c-567e-b3ee-8b8aa7b327bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500633} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde80f5-0a17-51cc-8fcb-e28702d3897c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500785} {:answer/id #uuid "e33da50a-6797-5e61-8dad-558084c1c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e33da50a-6797-5e61-8dad-558084c1c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f20aec0-879d-5670-bfea-c9c0def60bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b5c204f-7839-5ec6-95c0-a9151439158a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97637" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,1i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123f9b2b-0563-52eb-a8a6-c11e35140af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500507} {:answer/id #uuid "31c4c2e2-f9c7-55fa-9b0b-965e7a60983f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500570} {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} {:answer/id #uuid "d4901436-e9f5-5dcb-ad95-a9bd329e5817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500920} {:answer/id #uuid "ee63a80b-6b4b-5e42-89f8-7c9624056a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} :answer-field/id #uuid "360e3f58-2f52-52b9-85ca-0ee548100f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6798c7d5-6dc4-50ce-934a-27d8986f8abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-116498" :question/problem "![](images/32/324e3b2752fd398e47ce066dfc44f8a4d96cc3d7916f917fa142485ae86bf48f.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the height is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the surface area increasing when the dimensions of the rectangular prism are $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $7\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =7\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x,$ $y$ and $z.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2xz+2xy+2yz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+2y)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+(2x+2z)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+(2z+2y)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4)(-5)+(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7)(11)+(2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4)(-5) \\\\  & =-60+198-110 \\\\  & =28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the solid is increasing at a rate of $28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eec3fb3-98bb-539f-828a-96203367f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln(3)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eec3fb3-98bb-539f-828a-96203367f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln(3)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500764} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bfa90a3-ba24-559f-9c8b-5a07e671e7fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501023} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cf7146-ed56-5694-a708-a9b3016a6fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11cf7146-ed56-5694-a708-a9b3016a6fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500506} :answer-field/id #uuid "504d5d52-6b4f-57e5-9c75-2dca13703f87" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc780555-5bb0-55aa-b97f-a47fa4610bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc780555-5bb0-55aa-b97f-a47fa4610bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500860} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dc9af4-ea47-5fb1-8da7-ddafaed28423" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87416abb-dad9-5afe-bb9d-e8fcd5a9095c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350227" :question/problem "Given that  $f(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{6}{t}\\,k,$   what is ${f}^{′}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t))\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{6}{t}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 1)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\,j-\\frac{6}{{1}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f044fe5-a78a-503c-8136-573bff49033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500531} {:answer/id #uuid "2903205c-6d62-59f7-9321-c5aced82f76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500545} {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd5b608-b1ff-5a9d-9abd-12c753eba961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500731} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f3eb9-f956-5d80-bd59-824978a5c588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60c8b13-b572-54f8-b997-4b367f683cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a02e865a-6a6d-5b10-8171-4b544dbd46b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-2795" :question/problem "![](images/52/52448daf1f9aea06b4ab4713756080ddb3ab465b4fae8f92b65a54c251a81d5e.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $15\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ y & =5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ z & =15\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-50{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+xz\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+yz\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =(10)(5)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+(10)(15)(1)+(15)(5)(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500666} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} {:answer/id #uuid "c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500891} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-66523" :question/problem "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3}\\right)}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85efc12e-6686-5c83-a584-51d24265e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500745} {:answer/id #uuid "87c47d54-c33c-5791-b51c-9f273e7288ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500752} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d23ab-bc7a-5e58-9183-a23a3316f17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff089ad5-5112-577e-a4ad-ebbdd0861706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{(x-6)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e91312-58c2-59dc-9a23-0a2685890965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aef8db66-e528-55a5-877b-5de5c3121fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140270" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970} :answer-field/id #uuid "4367237c-774c-57e1-b55c-8026f7a5b4ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b06ab3d9-3b8c-5c9f-a694-75f899defb02" :question/math-academy-id "q-314513" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b45121fc7e3b6de3e37053b944bed610e55d337b7c73d89b258a89f903ad0c2.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $1.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height is increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $9{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ how fast is its width decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{feet}$ in length, $3\\,\\text{feet}$ in width, and $2\\,\\text{feet}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of width is positive.\n\nThe width is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+xz\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+yz\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d83943b-fa05-5a32-815b-4e9cc7e9a241" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7cf4a-4ced-579f-b3b1-a8420309518b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7e8649-8e07-5c84-b1f7-d22b5da0ebf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "badd637f-464d-556b-9b5c-ae357a07836a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218760" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}-\\,{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500651} {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500797} {:answer/id #uuid "c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500878} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144820" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d6b89b-9c2e-5338-b504-d4c40283ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "820{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500518} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2ce6b1-c4fc-5ed8-8a4c-3a4c5f749dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "525{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500526} {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9531f-3fb7-5eed-9a5e-1179bfcc6d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,750{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500755} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a2b408-e5cd-52e5-aaa7-f0316c914927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "670{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d21bae5-4b6c-54e9-aab3-a599790bd158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd9599f1-ed67-5948-967c-4c445ad154d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-49012" :question/problem "![](images/06/06eb8269e36ecb0185c0bfa0069c36a705e36711ca0a705fba3e6eae7b6c946d.png)\n\nJohn is a carpenter who wants to modify a wooden box. The length, width and height of the box are decreased at a rate of $1\\text{ cm/s},$ $2\\text{ cm/s}$ and $4\\text{ cm/s},$ respectively. Determine the rate at which the volume of the box decreases, if its dimensions are $15\\text{ cm}$ long, $10\\text{ cm}$ wide and $15\\text{ cm}$ high." :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=15\\text{ cm},\\, & W=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=15\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+L\\cdot H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+L\\cdot W\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(10)(15)(-1)+(15)(15)(-2)+(15)(10)(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the box is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0863c307-3fc4-50bb-8193-2637b4d1fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500482} {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c7a4-9e6e-5b6f-85e7-5e7350d7fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500565} {:answer/id #uuid "c0622a2f-34d6-58c4-b16a-d4b9c8c6f07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500866} {:answer/id #uuid "efca88fd-4fef-5d52-a234-b3f67f03da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fd739d-61ce-572d-ba84-147f6daa016b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e44f5e7f-50c8-5f61-b0b8-699aaec6f842" :question/math-academy-id "q-52504" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ given that $y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{y}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\ &= |\\frac{3}{y}| \\\\ &= \\frac{|3|}{|y|} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y<0.$ To take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|y|=-y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{|y|}=-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b852a09c-ea6b-5010-b9b3-a3b637cbd328" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e15d3e-925b-56d4-bf57-253dd32acaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343194" :question/problem "![](images/99/996768e4b1ce8ae57bcf3cce61059f8349c307ea9f05c9c1f51831ff6fd8ae68.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed. The length of the loaf is decreasing by $20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},$ while the $60\\,\\text{mm}$ width does not change. If the volume of the loaf is constant, how fast is its height changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =100\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ y & =60\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ z & =30\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =0\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is not changing and $y=60,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=60xz.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60xz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =60x\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+60z\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =60(100)\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+60(30)(-20) \\\\ 0 & =6000\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)-36\\,000 \\\\ 36\\,000 & =6000\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the solid is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500621} {:answer/id #uuid "610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500665} {:answer/id #uuid "663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500680} {:answer/id #uuid "cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500911} {:answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :question/math-academy-id "q-160895" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,-2),$ $(3,-1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00561303-4d8b-5648-aef1-ded86fb68a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500470} {:answer/id #uuid "15ab2670-044a-519f-a892-d7c304eec4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500515} {:answer/id #uuid "c588653b-6c79-5422-bfe3-23e763ffffb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500885} {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} {:answer/id #uuid "f65885a6-a87b-5af6-b23f-932e21b5918e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{93}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d040eed3-515d-5c40-bf7b-ad0d3ff29d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa23e3b-bd80-5fbc-b343-c6e4d7d3776d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140420" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-7x-11$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
